#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088942 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **complex roots**. Let's see how to find the solution in this case.\n\nWe will solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y =0.\n$$\n\n**Step 1:** Find the characteristic equation.\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ we find the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the ODE gives the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0\\,(\\text{since }{e}^{\\lambda x}\\ne 0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for the characteristic roots.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are a *complex conjugate pair*. Since the roots are distinct, the general solution is a linear combination of the corresponding exponentials:\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2\\textrm{i}x} + Be^{-2\\textrm{i}x}.\n$$\n\nOn the next slide, we will use *Euler's formula* to convert this into a more useful real-valued form." :tutorial/id #uuid "18a414e1-5fae-5260-8980-5971fbd844e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4052 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088943 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE may have complex solutions. In such cases, the general solution will initially involve complex exponentials.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 18y =0.\n$$\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}$, we get the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 18 =0.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by *completing the square*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two complex roots are $\\lambda_1 = 3 + 3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2 = 3 - 3\\textrm{i}.$\n\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{(3 + 3 \\textrm{i})x} + Be^{(3 - 3 \\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we will use *Euler's formula* to rewrite this solution in a form that does not involve complex numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "d2be766f-b054-5431-aaa5-32c2129b9d92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4053 :tutorial/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088957 :tutorial/content "In general, if the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **imaginary roots** \n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\pm b\\textrm{i},\n$$\n\nthen the *general solution* is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{\\textrm{i} b x} + B e^{-\\textrm{i} bx}.\n$$\n\nUsing *Euler's formula*, the above can be simplified to\n\n$$\ny(x) = P \\cos bx + Q \\sin bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $P = A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i} (A-B).$\n\nLet's take a look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0cc2f848-e126-5ee8-84ea-809e08a92dff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4162 :tutorial/title "A Shortcut When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088958 :tutorial/content "In general, if the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **complex conjugate roots** \n\n$$\n\\lambda = a \\pm b\\textrm{i},\n$$\n\n then the *general solution* is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{(a+b\\textrm{i})x} + B e^{(a-b\\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nUsing *Euler's formula*, we can expand and group the terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{ax} \\left[ (A+B)\\cos bx + \\textrm{i}(A-B)\\sin bx \\right].\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{ax} \\left( P \\cos bx + Q \\sin bx \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P = A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i} (A-B).$\n\nLet's take a look at another example" :tutorial/id #uuid "922b0814-7e51-5155-9464-ccf52671087e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4163 :tutorial/title "A Shortcut When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088863 :tutorial/content "Recall that our general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{(3 + 3 \\textrm{i})x} + Be^{(3 - 3 \\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nOur goal is to simplify this to find the **real-valued general solution**. We can do this using **Euler's formula**, which states that \n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos{\\theta} + \\textrm{i}\\sin{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we use the law of exponents to separate the real and imaginary parts in the exponential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x}{e}^{3\\text{i}x}+B{e}^{3x}{e}^{-3\\text{i}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(A{e}^{3\\text{i}x}+B{e}^{-3\\text{i}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply Euler's formula with $\\theta = 3x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{3x}(A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}((A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants, we can define new arbitrary constants $P=A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i}(A-B).$ The general solution becomes:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y = e^{3x}(P\\cos{3 x} + Q\\sin{3 x}).}\n$$\n\nThis is the final form of the solution, written entirely with real-valued functions.\n\nSo, for example,\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{3x}(5\\cos{3 x} - \\sin{3 x}),\n$$\n\ncorresponding to $P=5$ and $Q=-1,$ is a solution of the given differential equation. On the other hand,\n\n$$\ny(x) =  e^{3x}(\\cos{5 x} - \\sin{3 x})\n$$\n\nis *not* a solution because it contains a function $e^{3x}\\cos{5x},$ which is not included in the above form representing the general solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "25452a20-5d48-511d-b978-9a5f1c2b8894" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14887 :tutorial/title "Convert the Complex Solution to a Real-Valued Solution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088864 :tutorial/content "Recall our solution from the previous slide:\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2\\textrm{i}x} + Be^{-2\\textrm{i}x}.\n$$\n\nWe use **Euler's formula**, which states \n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta,\n$$\n\n to rewrite the exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are typically solving for a real-world quantity, our final answer must be a real-valued function. For this to be true, the arbitrary constants $A$ and $B$ must be a complex conjugate pair ($B = \\overline{A}$). This implies that the following definitions yield *real* constants $P$ and $Q$:\n\n$$\nP = A+B \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q = \\textrm{i}(A-B).\n$$\n\nThis gives the **general real-valued solution**:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = P\\cos{2x} + Q\\sin{2x}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary real constants determined by initial conditions.\n\nSo, for example,\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\cos{2x} - 2\\sin{2x},\n$$\n\ncorresponding to $P=1$ and $Q=-2,$ is a solution of the given differential equation. On the other hand,\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\cos{2x} + \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis *not* a solution because it contains a function $\\sin{x},$ which is not included in the above form representing the general solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "b126cbc0-4b1c-5a98-9c99-005a476f53c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14888 :tutorial/title "Convert the Complex Solution to a Real-Valued Solution"}]}
